---
import CookbookShell from './CookbookShell.astro';
---

<CookbookShell
  title={`How BiblicalMeal Researches Food | BiblicalMeal Cookbook`}
>
  <section class="cookbook-content px-5 py-16 sm:px-8 sm:py-24">
    <h1 class="font-display text-h1 text-ink">
      How BiblicalMeal Researches Food
    </h1>

    <div class="prose-bm mt-8 max-w-2xl space-y-6 text-body text-ink-muted">
      <p>Every food site makes promises. Ours are deliberately narrow.</p>

      <p>
        BiblicalMeal publishes around three kinds of knowledge, and we keep them
        visibly separate wherever they appear.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Scripture</strong> &mdash; what ancient texts name, bless, or forbid.
        This is textual information, quoted carefully and cited by book, chapter,
        and verse. When an ingredient page shows Scripture references, that is this
        first kind of knowledge.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>History</strong> &mdash; what scholars believe people of the ancient
        Mediterranean world grew, traded, and ate, based on evidence such as archaeobotany,
        trade records, and classical writing. When this cookbook shows historical
        context, that claim rests on sources we have reviewed &mdash; never on atmosphere.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Modern cooking</strong> &mdash; what works in a contemporary kitchen
        today. When a recipe carries our name, it is exactly what the recipe card
        says it is: a modern dish inspired by biblical-world ingredients, unless the
        page explicitly states otherwise.
      </p>

      <p>
        This discipline slows us down. An ingredient profile stays marked as <em
          >in research</em
        > &mdash; sometimes for months &mdash; while we chase down primary material
        and decide how confident we can honestly be. You will see those badges across
        this cookbook. They are not decoration; they are our quality control running
        in public.
      </p>

      <p>
        The reward is trust. When BiblicalMeal tells you something about figs,
        olives, or a bowl of lentil stew, you should be able to see where the
        statement comes from &mdash; and everything else stays clearly labelled
        as inspiration, not fact.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</CookbookShell>
